  "raw": " assist in the coming of diagnosis of a disease. This is substantially different from the case of biological tests, in which the identification is made for differentiation between infectious and non-infectious agents. It will be appreciated that identification of whether an agent is infectious or not is important, for example, in order to make sure whether or not a branch of a forest is under danger of being affected by a European Crane flu endemic disease.\nGenerally in the case of biological methods, detection of an infectious agent is performed by the use of a more or less sensitive test such as a microanalysis using the nucleic acid of the organism (for example, a bacterial DNA or RNA) or by collecting a specimen which retains the antigenic lanthanide element of the infectious agent, e.g. crystallization of a protein, blood cell counting using colour, and so on. For example in the case of protein tests, antibodies to the diagnostic antibodies can be used directly as a highly sensitive test in reverse—diagn",
